WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:10.440
Here’s a question.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:17.919
What would you say is a drug trafficker’s
biggest dream?

00:00:17.919 --> 00:00:24.220
Okay, allow me to explain myself.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:29.339
We all have dreams-- you, me, politicians...
and of course, drug traffickers are no exception.

00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:35.870
Though it may be hard to believe, in the heart
of the ruthless and cruel drug trafficking

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:40.740
industry, there are human beings with names,
surnames – and in this case also nicknames

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.949
– who like anyone else, also have dreams.

00:00:42.949 --> 00:00:45.230
So let’s go back to my question...

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:48.820
What’s the biggest dream a drug trafficker
could have?

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.790
I mean, of course there might be many possible
answers--we’re all individuals.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.829
But unfortunately... history tells us that
if most drug traffickers could make a wish,

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:01.820
just one wish... it’d be to turn their countries
into true narco-states.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.140
That is to say, they would turn them into
states run by a kind of criminal corporation

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.180
made up of politicians, public officials,
judges, the police, and the military, which,

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:13.310
in one way or another, were associated with
drugs.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:17.710
That way, drug traffickers could basically
act with impunity, exporting and distributing

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.530
narcotics without restriction.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.640
Very recently, we spoke here, on VISUALPOLITIK,
about how the Bolivarian regime of Nicolás

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.090
Maduro, Diosdado Cabello and the “Cartel
de los Soles” was turning Venezuela into

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.090
a narco-state.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.340
However, in Latin America, narco-politics
go far beyond Venezuela.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:38.340
In this video we’ll find out a bit more
about how one character was able to promote

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.790
the alliance between the narcotics industry
and politicians.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:42.790
Listen up.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:53.079
(My best friends are in the Tugurios Community,
in the municipal dump)

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:56.670
(NARCOPOPULISM)

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.460
We all know it: the narcotics industry is
one of the most important illegal businesses.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.240
Each year it moves several hundred billion
dollars.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.570
Now, allow me to ask another question, do
you think that a cartel can survive without

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.609
political support?

00:02:10.609 --> 00:02:13.480
Without controlling the institutions in any
way?

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.760
To answer this question, let's go back to
the beginning and take a look at how the most

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.760
important drug-dealers of all time managed
to do it...

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.349
Because even though it’s true that in the
beginning, drug traffickers started out as

00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:29.580
little more than a band of outlaws, the time
came when all of this changed...

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.870
A time... and a protagonist:

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:40.230
Pablo Escobar Gaviria, considered by Forbes
Magazine to be one of the richest men of his

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.300
time on the entire planet.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:47.200
According to Forbes, in 1987, Pablo Escobar
Gaviria, had a fortune that exceeded 2 billion

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.200
dollars at the time…

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.160
And Pablo Escobar radically changed the global
drug industry.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:58.500
See, on the one hand, he created an entire
network, a criminal holding company that included

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.599
“business” like extortion, kidnapping,
hired killers and, of course, the main activity,

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:04.599
drug-trafficking.

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:09.319
All these activities were also perfectly integrated
into a structure, the Medellin Cartel, which

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:16.250
had a clear organizational chart, a well-defined
hierarchy, and even a zone-based distribution

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:19.489
network… each with its own managers.

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:22.890
Something like franchises or branches.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.230
On the other hand, regarding his main business...
drugs… this Cartel, the Medellín Cartel,

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:30.510
achieved an almost complete vertical integration.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.989
That is, the Cartel took over the harvest,
production, distribution and marketing of

00:03:34.989 --> 00:03:36.790
the vast majority of its merchandise.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:43.519
They did everything, took over every stage
of the business.

00:03:43.519 --> 00:03:50.599
That’s how the Medellín Cartel managed
to invoice more than 7 billion dollars per

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:51.599
year.

00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:54.560
Of course... none of this would’ve been
possible without the complicity of the public

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:55.720
authorities.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:00.690
( “Apparently Escobar reached the top of
the cocaine cartel by astutely reinvesting

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:06.400
his initial earnings and becoming a PR master
in Colombia [...] With his growing wealth,

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.879
Escobar bought a newspaper in Medellín, gained
influence in Colombian politics and he successfully

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:16.159
ran for public office, becoming an alternative
to the national congress.”

00:04:16.159 --> 00:04:22.360
David Henry, Forbes Magazine 1987.)

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:29.409
See, for many years Pablo Escobar had impunity.

00:04:29.409 --> 00:04:33.860
With his money and threats – the well-known
phrase of silver or lead – he managed to

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:35.909
infiltrate the Colombian State.

00:04:35.909 --> 00:04:40.760
He bribed the military, politicians, governors,
the police... in such a way that many Colombian

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:44.169
institutions were under his command for a
long time.

00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:45.169
And that's not all.

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:49.649
Pablo Escobar also financed electoral campaigns,
and to win popular support – What does a

00:04:49.649 --> 00:04:54.659
politician do when he wants to gain popularity
fast – he promoted projects such as building

00:04:54.659 --> 00:04:59.719
houses for the nation’s poorest citizens,
distributing provisions, and creating soccer

00:04:59.719 --> 00:05:02.200
fields.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.800
Sounds good, doesn’t it?

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.360
At first glance... a Colombian Robin Hood.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.070
Too bad the truth was so different.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:16.210
While he was carrying out this type of campaign,
he had no qualms about flooding the streets

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.230
with violence and blood.

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:19.520
Welcome to Narcopopulism.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.729
The case is that, as you all know...

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:27.080
Pablo Escobar... managed to hold a seat in
the Colombian Parliament in 1982.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:31.339
Until then, thanks to his “political influences”
and his PR strategies, he was able to build

00:05:31.339 --> 00:05:35.440
his empire and his huge fortune with the utmost
impunity.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.690
(AUDIO: “A member of the United States Drug
Enforcement Agency once said that importing

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:46.140
exotic animals for his zoo caused Escobar
more problems with the government than his

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:47.330
drug business.”

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:48.330
David Henry)

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:49.330
Now... just a second.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:54.039
Don’t think that Pablo Escobar’s “political
relations” were limited to Colombia, not

00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:55.039
at all.

00:05:55.039 --> 00:05:59.070
See… after being elected as deputy... the
situation became untenable.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:03.130
Some members of the media began to tell the
truth hiding behind his fortune; the DEA’s

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:08.640
pressures multiplied, a lot of Colombian society
understood the huge danger that Pablo Escobar

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:14.100
entailed and other cartels began to fear that
the power of Medellín would grow even more.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:19.070
And that's when the government began to persecute
this capo... which declared war on the Colombian

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:20.430
state itself.

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:27.430
And just in case, the first thing Escobar
did was leave the country, lest he be hunted.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:33.370
(THE LEAP)

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:39.550
So Pablo Escobar put some space between himself
and Colombia.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:42.560
The question is, where could he find refuge?

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.720
Would his drug-money be enough to strengthen
alliances?

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:48.630
Well… it seems so.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:49.890
First of all, Panama...

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:56.630
At that time, the military dictator Manuel
Antonio Noriega offered him asylum, protection

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:59.140
and a route to transport his merchandise.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:05.560
Imagine, this industry was so powerful that
even dictators were willing to sell their

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:06.560
services.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.659
The fact is that, as you can see, Noriega
also turned Panama into a narco-state, until

00:07:11.659 --> 00:07:15.990
in 1989, a lightning invasion by the United
States removed him from power...

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:19.110
Detained and transferred to the United States,
Noriega was sentenced to 30 years in prison

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:24.279
for his crimes in drug-trafficking... and
his relationship with the Medellin Cartel.

00:07:24.279 --> 00:07:29.520
But... no, Pablo Escobar Gaviria’s external
alliances didn’t end here.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.149
The truth is that he didn’t feel very safe
in Panama...

00:07:32.149 --> 00:07:38.339
Let's see, yes, Noriega was a pal so to speak…
but the CIA had a big presence in the country...

00:07:38.339 --> 00:07:42.700
so when he least expected it, Noriega could
be tempted to sell him out.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:46.630
Because, of course, it was one thing to do
business with a Panamanian dictator and quite

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:48.970
another to entrust him with his protection.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:51.760
He needed to find a new destination.

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:54.800
So through the left-wing Colombian guerrilla
Movement, on April 19, better known as M-19,

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.019
Pablo Escobar contacted the Nicaraguan government.

00:07:58.019 --> 00:08:04.039
At that moment, the Sandinista Government
Board, led by the country’s current president

00:08:04.039 --> 00:08:09.500
Daniel Ortega, was willing to welcome him
with open arms in exchange for a commission...

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:10.720
you know, for services rendered.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.500
The agreement specified that, in addition
to settling in the country with his family,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:19.270
Pablo could use some of Nicaragua’s regions
as a drug-distribution platform.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:20.270
The problem?

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:24.409
Ortega and his friends were very greedy...
so a few months later Pablo decided to return

00:08:24.409 --> 00:08:25.409
to Colombia.

00:08:25.409 --> 00:08:28.800
But of course, he maintained his business
relations.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.380
But, there’s more.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.890
Along with Nicaragua, the M-19 guerrilla also
facilitated the Medellin Cartel’s contact

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:37.440
with the Cuban government…

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:43.840
Yes, you heard that right, Cuba was also a
trading platform for Pablo Escobar's businesses.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.180
In fact, it’s known that Pablo Escobar maintained
good relations with the Castro brothers.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.030
Although, truthfully, it seems that he wasn’t
the only one.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:55.330
All evidence indicates that after Escobar,
other drug-dealers continued to use Cuba,

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:59.130
through the Castros, to introduce all types
of narcotics into the United States.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.030
Anyway, Colombia, Panama, Nicaragua, Cuba...

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:06.410
As you can see, political power was one of
the main drivers of the huge presence of drug

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:07.910
trafficking in Latin America.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:12.100
The lack of transparency, corruption, lack
of counterweights and weak institutions were

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:16.370
a perfect breeding ground for what over the
years became one of the great nightmares of

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:17.370
the region.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:22.270
And yes, some of you may be thinking... well,
okay Simon... but in the end Pablo Escobar

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.270
fell from power.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:28.931
It's true, but when a capo falls, it doesn’t
mean that the industry falls… nor do their

00:09:28.931 --> 00:09:30.840
relations with political power.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:33.480
Let's take a look at Mexico.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:37.370
(THE RELAY)

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:45.721
The main Mexican cartel with which Pablo Escobar
had a relationship was the Guadalajara Cartel,

00:09:45.721 --> 00:09:51.780
led by Ernesto Fonseca, Rafael Caro Quintero
and Miguel Ángel Felix Gallardo.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:56.860
This cartel had full impunity for 2 decades...
all thanks to their relations with people

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:57.860
in power.

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:01.500
See, Felix Gallardo was in charge of “public
relations”... and thanks to his ability

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:05.990
to move within the highest political spheres,
this Cartel had all the stability it needed

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:07.510
to grow and develop.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:11.380
PR is so important!

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:19.010
However, we’re talking about a criminal
industry, and in criminal organizations stability

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.990
is very hard to achieve.

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:25.570
In this case, the assault on the DEA agent,
Enrique Camarena, which ended his life, made

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:30.150
Washington demand the dismantling of this
cartel... and its leaders fell one by one.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:32.330
But... this didn’t end the business.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:36.830
From prison, in 1989, Felix Gallardo tried
to reorganize the territories... but in the

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:39.800
absence of an agreement the Cartel split in
two:

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:44.740
The Tijuana Cartel led by the Arellano brothers
and the Sinaloa Cartel led by Héctor Luis

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:50.760
Palma Salazar “El güero Palma” and Joaquín
Guzmán Loera, aka “el Chapo”, who debuted

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:58.240
on the Forbes list in 2009 with an estimated
fortune of more than 1 billion dollars.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:02.300
The fact is that under el Chapo’s command,
the Sinaloa Cartel became the most important

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:07.210
and influential cartel in Mexico and one of
the most important criminal organizations

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:08.210
in the world.

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:10.430
The question that many of you may be asking
is…

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:11.430
How did he do it?

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:14.840
How can one Cartel grow so much more than
its adversaries?

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:17.740
Well, above all... because of political influences.

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:22.160
For example, how do you prevent the federal
police from catching you and dismantling your

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:23.160
business?

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.980
Well... by for example finding out when those
raids will happen.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:30.730
Everything is based on good relations... and
that’s precisely what El Chapo made sure

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:31.730
he’d have.

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:33.950
Information and “friends” are power.

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:39.060
And, as we’ve seen in other cases, El Chapo’s
capture didn’t really change the panorama.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:43.740
Today, the Sinaloa Cartel is controlled by
Ismael “el Mayo” Zambada, who, over the

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:47.650
years, has been known to buy officials from
the Special Prosecutor's Office, the Attorney

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:53.060
General's Office and even the Counter-Narcotics
Intelligence Center, an elite army corps created

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:54.210
to fight narcos.

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:59.370
(“Obviously they don’t do these businesses
alone, in many cases they have the approval

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:05.880
or participation of the authorities”, F.J.R
researcher at the University of Guadalajara)

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:09.779
All in complicity with senior government officials.

00:12:09.779 --> 00:12:15.060
Only that explains these criminal groups’
power, strength and resilience.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:19.680
We’re talking about a political power with
which, in many cases, narcos create a symbiosis

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:20.900
that is hard to distinguish.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:24.820
That’s what’s happening in Venezuela with
the Soles Cartel, of which we’ve already

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:25.820
spoken here on VISUALPOLITIK.

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:29.980
And watch out, because it’s estimated that
the Mexican and Venezuelan cartels control

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:33.660
70% of all the drugs that enter the United
States.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:38.751
That’s what happens in countries like Guatemala,
where some sources state that up to 80% of

00:12:38.751 --> 00:12:43.720
all the white powder could be mediated by
members of the national army itself; or what’s

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:48.190
happening in Honduras, where even the son
of former President Porfirio Lobo has been

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:52.260
condemned in the United States for drug trafficking
activities while his father, his own father,

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:53.810
was President of the Republic.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:58.430
Anyway, folks, that’s the result of weak
institutions, a lack of transparency and a

00:12:58.430 --> 00:12:59.430
lack of counterweights.

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:03.460
On VISUALPOLITIK we’ll talk much more about
this issue which is becoming very expensive

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:04.930
for many Latin American countries.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:09.270
With this video we wanted to show you a few
examples of how some of the great narcos “succeeded”

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:13.120
so well, largely thanks to their influence
on established institutions.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:16.880
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:18.490
subscribe for brand new videos.

